;; A colour written into a trait signature.
;;
;; Refused at the declaration, and that is the point of the fixture: it used to
;; be *accepted* and mean nothing. `resolveTypeAnnotation` carries the effect
;; into the trait's stored signature faithfully, and then every consumer drops
;; it — an impl method's outermost effect is overwritten from its own definer,
;; and the emitted interface spells the return type without asking what colour
;; it is.
;;
;; What made silence untenable is that the messages downstream lead into each
;; other. Calling such a method said "an ordinary function cannot be used where
;; a bjoroutine is expected. Define it with defbjo", and defining the impl
;; method with defbjo said "a trait signature has no way to say that calling a
;; method suspends" — a sentence about a program whose trait signature had just
;; said exactly that. Three messages, and the last contradicted the first.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: declares 'fetch' with -bjo->, and a trait method cannot carry a colour yet
;; EXPECT-ERROR: has to know it is a yield point before it knows which implementation it reached

(import (std prelude))

(def/trait (Fetcher %a)
  (: fetch (-bjo-> %a string)))

(def/impl (Fetcher int)
  (defun (fetch x) "hi"))

(defun (main args) 0)
